          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best hot tub for the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people trying a hot tub, a good inflatable like the square Coleman SaluSpa Hawaii AirJet offers the most for the money, with room for four to six and an efficiency-focused cover. If you want a hard shell without hiring an electrician, a plug-and-play tub like the Lifesmart LS200 is the value step up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are plug-and-play hot tubs any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, within their limits. They run on 120V, usually on a dedicated 15 or 20 amp circuit, so they heat more slowly and many cannot run the heater at full power with the jets on high. For one to five people who keep the tub covered at temperature, they work well and avoid 240V wiring.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run a hot tub?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends mostly on weather, insulation and the cover. As an example, a 1,500-watt heater running eight hours a day uses 12 kWh, about $2 a day at 17 cents per kWh. A well-insulated tub with a good locking cover runs its heater much less.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What temperature should a hot tub be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The CPSC recommends that hot tub water never exceed 104°F. Many people are comfortable between 100 and 102°F. Use lower temperatures for children and shorter sessions at the top of the range, and ask a doctor first if you are pregnant or have a heart condition.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a hot tub on my deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the deck is built for it. A filled tub with people can exceed the roughly 40 lb per square foot live load many residential decks are designed for. Have a builder or engineer check the structure, or put the tub on a concrete pad or compacted paver base instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do inflatable hot tubs last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With careful use, an inflatable often lasts a few seasons. Punctures, pump wear and UV damage are the usual causes of failure. Keeping it covered, on a smooth mat away from sharp objects, and draining and storing it properly in winter all extend its life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do hot tubs need a dedicated circuit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most do. Plug-and-play tubs typically need a dedicated 15 or 20 amp 120V circuit with GFCI protection, and the Lifesmart LS500 Plus, for example, is listed as needing a dedicated 15-amp circuit. 240V tubs need a dedicated circuit and GFCI disconnect installed by a licensed electrician.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

